You notice it mid-manicure, usually. Light catches the nail at an angle you don't normally look at it from, and there they are: fine lines running from cuticle to tip, like the surface quietly changed texture without asking. Here's the reassurance most articles bury under a wall of disease names: <strong>vertical ridges on nails</strong> are, in the vast majority of cases, just aging. Not illness. Not a deficiency crisis. Just your nails doing what skin and hair also do over time. That said, "usually" is not "always." <strong>Vertical ridges on nails causes</strong> range from completely benign to worth a doctor's visit, and the rest of this comes down to which one applies to you.

<hr className="my-8 border-gray-200" />

## Are Vertical Ridges on Nails Normal?

Yes, mostly. Vertical ridges are one of the most common nail changes there is, and on their own, they are not a warning sign. If your nails are otherwise a consistent colour, not swollen or painful, and the lines run neatly from base to tip, you are looking at the ordinary texture change that comes with nail aging, not something your body is trying to flag.

That reframe matters because the internet tends to lead with the scary version first. Search "why do I have ridges on my nails" and you land on lists mentioning thyroid disease and arthritis before the word "aging" even appears. In reality, aging accounts for the overwhelming majority of cases. The exceptions exist, and we will get to exactly what pairs with a ridge to shift it from cosmetic to clinical. For now: noticing a few faint vertical lines on nails you've had your whole life is not a diagnosis. It's a Tuesday.

<hr className="my-8 border-gray-200" />

## What Causes Vertical Ridges on Nails

<strong>Vertical ridges on nails causes</strong> come down to changes in how quickly and evenly your nail cells regenerate. Your nail is not one solid piece. It's built from layers of keratin cells that form at the matrix, under your cuticle, and get pushed forward as new cells form behind them. When that cell turnover becomes slightly uneven, which happens naturally over time, the nail plate stops growing perfectly flat and starts growing in fine parallel ridges instead.

The medical term for this is <strong>onychorrhexis</strong>, a general word for brittle, ridged, or splitting nails. The <strong>nail matrix</strong> is the engine room behind all of it: the tissue under the base of the nail responsible for producing new cells. Anything that slows or disrupts that production shows up, eventually, as texture on the surface.

Age is the leading cause, full stop. Keratin production naturally slows as we get older, and the nail plate grows thinner and less flexible as a result. Dehydration compounds this. Frequent hand washing, cold weather, and acetone-based polish removers strip the natural oils that keep the nail plate pliable, so ridges already forming become easier to see and feel. Nutrient gaps play a supporting role too. Low iron, zinc, or protein intake can slow keratin production further, and this is one area where <cite index="27-1">a lone vertical ridge is almost never a thyroid sign, it's usually just dehydration or age</cite>, according to health data reviewed by clinical researchers. Skin conditions like eczema on the fingers can also create temporary vertical lines that resolve once the skin calms. Repeated mechanical stress, harsh manicures, acetone soaks, aggressive filing, can etch grooves into the matrix that surface as ridging months later. If what you're seeing feels bigger than ridges alone, it may be worth reading up on <a href='/blog/nail-strengthening/why-are-my-nails-weak-and-brittle'>the broader causes of weak and brittle nails</a>, since the two often travel together.

<hr className="my-8 border-gray-200" />

## Why Nail Ridges Get More Noticeable With Age

Your thirties are usually where <strong>vertical ridges on nails</strong> become visible. Not because anything is wrong, but because keratin production, like collagen production in skin, gradually slows on a schedule that has nothing to do with your health habits. Nail cell turnover that once ran smoothly starts to lose a bit of consistency, and the nail plate itself becomes thinner and slightly less flexible with each growth cycle.

This is also why ridges worsen gradually rather than appear overnight. A nail takes roughly three to six months to grow out completely, so what you're seeing today reflects months of cell turnover, not something that happened last week. By your forties and fifties, most people have some degree of visible ridging, and dermatology sources describe it as a normal part of the aging nail rather than a cosmetic failure. Hormonal shifts, particularly around menopause, can accelerate this, which is part of why ridges are sometimes nicknamed "nail wrinkles." For the fuller picture past this stage of life, <a href='https://mirelleinspo.com/blog/nails-for-older-women/nail-care-routine-for-mature-hands'>the full daily nail care routine dermatologists recommend for nails after 50</a> goes deeper into what actually helps.

