## When to See a Doctor About Nail Ridges

See a doctor about <strong>vertical ridges on nails</strong> if they arrive suddenly and severely, come with a colour change, cause pain or swelling, affect every nail at once with noticeable thickening, or show up alongside symptoms like fatigue or hair thinning that suggest something systemic. Any one of those on its own is worth mentioning at your next appointment. Several together warrant booking sooner rather than later.

Here's the decision framework, in order. First, check direction: horizontal ridges are more concerning than vertical, since they signal interrupted growth rather than gradual aging. Second, check colour: healthy nails stay one even tone, so white, yellow, dark, or green discoloration alongside ridging is a reason to get checked, according to <a href='https://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/adult-health/expert-answers/nails/faq-20058541'>Mayo Clinic</a>. Third, check scope: ridges on every nail, paired with thickness or brittleness, fit the pattern seen with thyroid conditions more than ordinary aging. Fourth, check company: pain, swelling, or a nail lifting from surrounding skin is never "just aging." If none of those apply, you're looking at the harmless kind, and no appointment is necessary.

<hr className="my-8 border-gray-200" />

## The Nails You Actually Have

Vertical ridges are not a verdict on your health. They're closer to the fine lines that show up on skin eventually, a sign of years lived rather than a body in distress. Most people will develop them, most will never need to think twice about them, and the ones who do have a clear checklist now instead of a vague sense of dread borrowed from a symptom list online.

<strong>Vertical ridges on nails</strong> respond to care even when they can't be erased. Keep the nail plate hydrated, buff sparingly, reach for a ridge-filling base coat on the days it matters, and save the worry for the signs that actually warrant it: colour change, pain, or ridges that showed up on every nail overnight. Everything else is just your hands, aging the way hands do.
